<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day08" xml:id="day-08-lecture">
    <title>Day 08</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            More Linear Regression
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
        <title>Covered in this class</title>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Qualitative predictors with more than two levels
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Extending the linear model with interaction terms
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Hierarchy principle
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Polynomial regression
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs xml:id="more-than-two-levels">
        <title>Qualitative Predictor with More than Two Levels</title>

        <sidebyside>
            <table xml:id="tab-day08-region">
                <title>Region</title>

                <tabular>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell><m>x_{i1}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>x_{i2}</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>South</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>West</cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>East</cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    Create spare dummy variables:
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        x_{i1}=
              \begin{cases}
              1 &amp; \text{if}\,i\text{th person from South}\\
              0 &amp; \text{if}\,i\text{th person not from South}
              \end{cases}
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        x_{i2}=
              \begin{cases}
              1 &amp; \text{if}\,i\text{th person from West}\\
              0 &amp; \text{if}\,i\text{th person not from West}
              \end{cases}
                    </md>

                    <note>
                        <p>
                            The level with no dummy variable (East here) is
                            known as <term>the baseline</term>.
                        </p>
                    </note>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <md>
                y_i=\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\epsilon_i
          =
          \begin{cases}
          \beta_0+\beta_1+\epsilon_i &amp;
          \text{if}\,i\text{th person from South}\\
          \beta_0+\beta_2+\epsilon_i &amp;
          \text{if}\,i\text{th person from West}\\
          \beta_0+\epsilon_i &amp;
          \text{if}\,i\text{th person from East}
          \end{cases}
            </md>
        </p>

        <exercise>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the interpretation for <m>\beta_0</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>\beta_0</m> can be interpreted as the average credit
                        card balance for individuals from the East.
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the interpretation for <m>\beta_1</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>\beta_1</m> can be interpreted as the difference in
                        the average balance between people from the South versus
                        the East.
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the interpretation for <m>\beta_2</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>\beta_2</m> can be interpreted as the difference in
                        the average balance between those from the West versus
                        the East.
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="more-on-multiple-levels">
        <title>More on multiple levels</title>

        <sidebyside>
            <stack>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Always one less variable than levels.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Level with no dummy variable is called the baseline.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <image source="figures/Table3-8.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Regression results for regional dummy variables.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Balance for the baseline (EAST) is $531.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            South has $18.69 less than east.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            West has $12.50 less than east.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>p</m>-values for dummy variables are large, so
                            can't reject null hypothesis.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            No statistical evidence that there's a difference in
                            CC balance in different regions.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Assume we are trying to build a regression model to predict
                    the price of a Pokemon card based on its grading (
                    <sq>Grading</sq> ).
                    The card grading levels are Near Mint ( <sq>NM</sq> ),
                    Lightly Played ( <sq>LP</sq> ), Heavily Played ( <sq>HP</sq>
                    ), and Damaged ( <sq>DMG</sq> ).
                    Answer the following questions.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How many dummy variables will we need to encode
                        <sq>Grading</sq>?
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        Since we have 4 levels, we will need 3 variables (one
                        less than the number of levels).
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Create a table similar to <xref ref="tab-day08-region"/>
                        using the ordering from top to bottom: <sq>NM</sq>,
                        <sq>LP</sq>, <sq>HP</sq>, <sq>DMG</sq>.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <tabular>
                        <row>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell><m>x_{i1}</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>x_{i2}</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>x_{i3}</m></cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>NM</cell>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>LP</cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>HP</cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>DMG</cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </answer>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Choosing <sq>DMG</sq> as the reference variable and
                        using 0/1 one-hot encoding, write the expressions for
                        all the dummy variables for the <m>i</m> th card.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <md>
                            <mrow>                        x_{i1}\amp=
              \begin{cases}
              1 &amp; \text{if}\,i\text{th card is NM}\\
              0 &amp; \text{otherwise}
              \end{cases} </mrow>
                            <mrow>                        x_{i2}\amp=
              \begin{cases}
              1 &amp; \text{if}\,i\text{th card is LP}\\
              0 &amp; \text{otherwise}
              \end{cases} </mrow>
                            <mrow>x_{i3}\amp=
              \begin{cases}
              1 &amp; \text{if}\,i\text{th card is HP}\\
              0 &amp; \text{otherwise}
              \end{cases} </mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write the piecewise form for predicting the price of the
                        <m>i</m> th card
                        <md>
                            y_i=\beta_0+\beta_1 x_{i1} + \ldots + \epsilon_i.
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <md>
                            <mrow>y_i\amp=\beta_0+\beta_1 x_{i1}+\beta_2 x_{i2}+\beta_3 x_{i3}+\epsilon_i</mrow>
                            <mrow>\amp=
          \begin{cases}
          \beta_0+\beta_1+\epsilon_i &amp;
          \text{if}\,i\text{th card is NM}\\
          \beta_0+\beta_2+\epsilon_i &amp;
          \text{if}\,i\text{th card is LP}\\
          \beta_0+\beta_3+\epsilon_i &amp;
          \text{if}\,i\text{th card is HP}\\
          \beta_0+\epsilon_i &amp;
          \text{if}\,i\text{th card is DMG}
          \end{cases}</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write down the interpretation for each coefficient in
                        your model, <m>\beta_0, \beta_1, \ldots, \beta_p</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <dl>
                            <li>
                                <title><m>\beta_0</m></title>

                                <p>
                                    Average price for DMG cards.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <title><m>\beta_1</m></title>

                                <p>
                                    Difference in the average price between NM
                                    and DMG cards.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <title><m>\beta_2</m></title>

                                <p>
                                    Difference in the average price between LP
                                    and DMG cards.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <title><m>\beta_3</m></title>

                                <p>
                                    Difference in the average price between HP
                                    and DMG cards.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </dl>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Using the following table, write down the values for
                        each of the coefficients in your regression model.
                    </p>

                    <table>
                        <title>Average card values versus grading</title>

                        <tabular>
                            <row>
                                <cell>Near Mint (NM)</cell>
                                <cell>$12.35</cell>
                            </row>

                            <row>
                                <cell>Lightly Played (LP)</cell>
                                <cell>$8.87</cell>
                            </row>

                            <row>
                                <cell>Heavily Played (HP)</cell>
                                <cell>6.72</cell>
                            </row>

                            <row>
                                <cell>Damaged (DMG)</cell>
                                <cell>$4.17</cell>
                            </row>
                        </tabular>
                    </table>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\beta_0 \amp=4.17 </mrow>
                            <mrow>\beta_1 \amp=(\beta_0+\beta_1)-\beta_0=12.35-4.17=$8.20 </mrow>
                            <mrow>\beta_2 \amp=(\beta_0+\beta_2)-\beta_0=8.87-4.17 =$4.80 </mrow>
                            <mrow>\beta_3 \amp=(\beta_0+\beta_3)-\beta_0=6.72-4.17 =$2.60</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Extending the linear model: interaction terms</title>

        <p>
            Assumptions so far (for Advertising data):
            <md>
                \hat Y_{sales}
	= \beta_0
	+ \beta_1 \cdot X_{{TV}}
	+ \beta_2 \cdot X_{{radio}}
	+ \beta_3 \cdot X_{{newspaper}}
            </md>
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="45%">
            <p>
                Assumed (implicitly) that the effect on sales by increasing one
                medium is independent of the others.
            </p>

            <p>
                What if spending money on radio advertising increases the
                effectiveness of TV advertising?
                How do we model it?
            </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            Interaction term
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="45%">
            <p>
                <md>
                    Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1X_1 + \beta_2 X_2 + \beta_3 X_1X_2 + \epsilon
                </md>
                <md>
                    <mrow>Y_{sales} \amp= \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_{TV} + \beta_2 X_{radio}+ \beta_3 X_{radio}X_{TV} + \epsilon</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= \beta_0 + (\beta_1 + \beta_3 X_{radio}) X_{TV} + \beta_2 X_{radio} + \epsilon</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp=\beta_0 + \tilde{\beta_1} X_{TV} + \beta_2 X_{radio} + \epsilon</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\beta_3 X_{\text{radio}} X_{\text{TV}}</m> is the
                            interaction term
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\tilde{\beta_1} = \beta_1 + \beta_3 X_{radio}</m>,
                            function of <m>X_2</m> association no longer
                            constant between <m>X_1</m> and <m>Y</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside width="45%">
            <p>
                <md>
                    Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1X_1 + \beta_2 X_2 + \beta_3 X_1X_2 + \epsilon
                </md>
                <md>
                    <mrow>Y_{sales} &amp;= \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_{TV} + \beta_2 X_{radio}+ \beta_3 X_{radio}X_{TV} + \epsilon</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= \beta_0 + (\beta_1 + \beta_3 X_{radio}) X_{TV} + \beta_2 X_{radio} + \epsilon</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <stack>
                <image source="figures/Table3-9.png" width="80%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Coefficients, standard error, t-statistic, and p-value
                        for Sales versus TV, radio, TV times radio, all in
                        thousands of dollars.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                P-value for interaction term is low, strong
                                evidence for <m>H_a: \beta_3 \neq 0</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>R^2</m> is 96.8% for this, vs 89.7% for model
                                without interaction
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\beta_3</m> the increase in the effectiveness
                                of TV advertising associated with a one-unit
                                increase in radio advertising
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            Interpretation.
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="45%">
            <image source="figures/Table3-9.png" width="80%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Coefficients, standard error, t-statistic, and p-value for
                    Sales versus TV, radio, TV times radio, all in thousands of
                    dollars.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            An increase of $1K in TV advertising is associated
                            with increased sales of
                        </p>
                        <md>
                            (\beta_1 + \beta_3 X_{\text{radio}}) \cdot 1000
      = 19 + 1.1 \cdot X_{\text{radio}}
                        </md>

                        <p>
                            units.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            An increase of $1K in radio advertising is
                            associated with increased sales of
                            <md>
                                (\beta_2 + \beta_3 X_{\text{TV}}) \cdot 1000
      = 29 + 1.1 \cdot X_{\text{TV}}
                            </md>
                            units.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Hierarchy principle</title>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="5%">
            <p>
                Sometimes the <m>p</m>-value for an interaction term is very
                small, but its associated main effects are not.
            </p>

            <p>
                The hierarchy principle:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        If we include an interaction term, we should include its
                        main effects regardless of their individual
                        significance.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Nonlinear relationships</title>

        <p>
            <md>
                \texttt{mpg}
    = \beta_0
    + \beta_1 \cdot \texttt{horsepower}
    + \beta_2 \cdot \texttt{horsepower}^2
    + \epsilon
            </md>
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="5%">
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        Quadratic shape of the data suggests the model above.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        It is still linear regression because we use
                        <m>X_1 = \texttt{horsepower}</m> and
                        <m>X_2 = \texttt{horsepower}^2</m>.
                    </li>

                    <li>We could add more terms, but risk overfitting.</li>
                    <li>
                        Polynomial regression is covered further in Chapter 7.
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>

            <stack>
                <image source="figures/Fig3-8.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Scatter plot of miles per gallon versus horsepower with
                        linear, degree 2, and degree 5 fits.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <image source="figures/Table3-10.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Coefficients, standard error, t-statistic, and p-value
                        for a quadratic polynomial fit of miles per gallon
                        versus horse power.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>
        Group work: Work in groups to complete
        <xref ref="activities" text="custom">Day08 in-class activity</xref>.
    </p>
</handout>